HST Office: the shared string pool keeps its strings in an open-addressing table, about half the memory.

svl::SharedStringPool (every text cell of Sheets, and the case-insensitive partners that sorting, filtering and
lookups compare) kept each string in a std::unordered_map node: one heap block of 48 bytes per string (next pointer,
key string, cached key hash, upper-case string, cached node hash) plus an 8-byte bucket pointer. A large workbook
(ketoan-50: 1.16 million distinct texts, 1.92 million pool entries with the upper-case strings) spends about
100 MB on these nodes and buckets, before any string data.

The pool now uses HstStringPoolTable (new hststringpooltable.cxx): linear probing over two flat arrays, the string and
its upper-case string (16 bytes) and the 32-bit string hash (4 bytes), load at most 4/5, growth by half (any
capacity: Fibonacci mixing, then multiply-shift onto the slot range). About 31 bytes per entry on average instead of
about 59; arrays this large are mmap'ed by malloc, so a growth returns the old array to the system instead of
leaving 48-byte holes between live strings. A lookup compares the string only when the hash matches.

Unchanged: what intern() returns (the same rtl_uString for equal strings, the shared upper-case string), the
references the pool holds (one on the string and one on its upper case per entry, so a string that is its own
upper case is held twice), and purge(): first entries whose string differs from its upper case and that only the
pool references, then upper-case entries that only their own entry references. After a purge the remaining entries
are moved back along their probe runs in one pass that starts at a slot that was free before the purge.

Hook: svl/source/misc/sharedstringpool.cxx (SharedStringPool::Impl holds the table; intern/purge/getCount call it),
svl/Library_svl.mk; logic in svl/source/inc/hststringpooltable.hxx, svl/source/misc/hststringpooltable.cxx.
Test: CppunitTest_svl_hst_string_pool (the pool's heap for 1,924,229 entries stays under 40 bytes per entry; 100,000
strings in three spellings, 300 strings with one hash, purges that keep every third string or every other one of the
same-hash run, then every string is found or comes back once; a model of the purge rules on a fixed random
sequence), CppunitTest_svl_qa_cppunit (the upstream pool tests).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new files are HST's (LicenseRef-HST-Proprietary).
